{; Verdict: State A stored both transaction-metadata values as refs.
 ; Keep that shape: provenance resolves to durable user/process entities,
 ; never denormalized strings on the transaction entity. The :seon.db/index
 ; facet admits both into canonical-database-attributes by the computed
 ; persistence-facet rule (a bare :seon.db/ref carries no facet and would
 ; never install).
 ; the :and wrap is the one idiom for facet properties on a referenced
 ; form — properties attach to Malli TYPES, and :seon.db/ref is a
 ; registered alias, not a type
 :seon.db/user [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/ref]
 :seon.db/process [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/ref]
 ; the process entity provenance refs resolve to; identities are the
 ; owner-ruled literal core set (:seon.db.process/{boot,config,core})
 ; seeded as GENESIS DATA in the ancestor, where bootstrap content lives
 :seon.db.process/id [:string {:min 1 :seon.db/identity true}]

 ; A RUN'S WHY IS TRANSACTION METADATA (owner ruling, 2026-07-27 night):
 ; the run-opening transaction carries a ref to the message that caused
 ; it, so "has this trigger been answered?" is "does any run-opening
 ; transaction point at it". The one deliberate extension of minimal
 ; provenance (user + process) on ONE transition — never copied onto
 ; the run entity, which is why this key is NOT :seon.cluster.run/*.
 ; Indexed like the other two: a bare ref never installs (the computed
 ; persistence-facet rule above), and "answered?" queries BY the ref.
 :seon.db/trigger [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/ref]}
